[[:db/retract 17592186194874 :answer-field/choices 17592186194875] [:db/retract 17592186194874 :answer-field/choices 17592186194876] [:db/retract 17592186194874 :answer-field/choices 17592186194877] {:db/id 17592186198457 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-33e4bfa0-0240-5076-a3cc-c397d31c6632" "question-0373eae5-d413-558b-aff8-967aba0289ff"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198102 :question/problem "Write down $\\lnot P(x)$ given that\n![](images/ed/ed810318e9b5d6e76b77735530ff9312af66f936d7c8e197d6dd0fb99227dcb8.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$\\neg$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n![](images/97/976e6fe9a107732c81210776e49cfe3f68939512c0fad63739e2f59acb17f109.svg) \n\nIn other words, $x$ is smaller than $10.$ Therefore, $\\neg P(x)$ means the following:\n![](images/dc/dca132f34f73e2f5987a803e592c1f664cc790ed83ec204205975c401280038c.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198458 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f94afce2-6e2a-536a-8567-c4c1dfc21cb5" "question-8f075ba4-7815-572a-b12a-c4691f9f8fab"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198459 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f501796-fc0b-5879-8eb1-568ded583cc8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-33e4bfa0-0240-5076-a3cc-c397d31c6632" :question/id #uuid "33e4bfa0-0240-5076-a3cc-c397d31c6632" :question/math-academy-id "q-175413" :question/problem "$\\,$ $x$ is smaller than or equal to $3.$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is smaller than or equal to $3.$\n\nIn other words, $x$ is greater than $3.$ Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is greater than $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b00b0b0b-3141-5bf4-8a83-5d11c6bf823a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b00b0b0b-3141-5bf4-8a83-5d11c6bf823a" :answer-field/id #uuid "b00b0b0b-3141-5bf4-8a83-5d11c6bf823a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c186e2f7-ceca-5bf5-a121-ec500999054d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c186e2f7-ceca-5bf5-a121-ec500999054d" "answer-f26ba7d0-f50d-5e14-abd0-b24c0635b31f" "answer-e4d99c01-33bd-5bc6-a18e-4cf6f884a4d2" "answer-24ec13d9-ba04-5cf2-871e-2d3da08d0af9" "answer-e3ff3d40-913d-5dda-9c9a-d4a87dacba6c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c186e2f7-ceca-5bf5-a121-ec500999054d" :answer/id #uuid "c186e2f7-ceca-5bf5-a121-ec500999054d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is greater than $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f26ba7d0-f50d-5e14-abd0-b24c0635b31f" :answer/id #uuid "f26ba7d0-f50d-5e14-abd0-b24c0635b31f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is greater than or equal to $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4d99c01-33bd-5bc6-a18e-4cf6f884a4d2" :answer/id #uuid "e4d99c01-33bd-5bc6-a18e-4cf6f884a4d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is not equal to $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24ec13d9-ba04-5cf2-871e-2d3da08d0af9" :answer/id #uuid "24ec13d9-ba04-5cf2-871e-2d3da08d0af9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is not greater than $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3ff3d40-913d-5dda-9c9a-d4a87dacba6c" :answer/id #uuid "e3ff3d40-913d-5dda-9c9a-d4a87dacba6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is smaller than $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0373eae5-d413-558b-aff8-967aba0289ff" :question/id #uuid "0373eae5-d413-558b-aff8-967aba0289ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-175412" :question/problem "$\\,$ $x$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nThe predicate $P(x,y)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nIn other words, $x$ is greater than or equal to $y.$ Therefore, $¬P(x,y)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is greater than or equal to $y.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b10d84a-ed52-5d15-bf1f-79a5856c3da0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b10d84a-ed52-5d15-bf1f-79a5856c3da0" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b10d84a-ed52-5d15-bf1f-79a5856c3da0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-68ae4d49-f3f0-502a-b9d7-38aa0c211660" :answer-field/choices ["answer-af5653ad-ac62-587a-bac4-a6048e331507" "answer-bad69646-8267-5dda-8461-557726caa010" "answer-68ae4d49-f3f0-502a-b9d7-38aa0c211660" "answer-b34e3514-08c0-5818-a19c-8df103450dd9" "answer-2ade7d61-5826-5d6e-8c44-465826c146fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af5653ad-ac62-587a-bac4-a6048e331507" :answer/id #uuid "af5653ad-ac62-587a-bac4-a6048e331507" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is equal to $y$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bad69646-8267-5dda-8461-557726caa010" :answer/id #uuid "bad69646-8267-5dda-8461-557726caa010" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is greater than $y$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68ae4d49-f3f0-502a-b9d7-38aa0c211660" :answer/id #uuid "68ae4d49-f3f0-502a-b9d7-38aa0c211660" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is greater than or equal to $y$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b34e3514-08c0-5818-a19c-8df103450dd9" :answer/id #uuid "b34e3514-08c0-5818-a19c-8df103450dd9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is not greater than $y$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ade7d61-5826-5d6e-8c44-465826c146fc" :answer/id #uuid "2ade7d61-5826-5d6e-8c44-465826c146fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ is smaller than $y$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f94afce2-6e2a-536a-8567-c4c1dfc21cb5" :question/id #uuid "f94afce2-6e2a-536a-8567-c4c1dfc21cb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-175313" :question/problem "$\\,$ $x≱\\pi $\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "In plain English, our predicate $P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is not greater than or equal to $\\pi .$\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is not greater than or equal to $\\pi .$\n\nIn other words, $x$ *is* *greater than or equal to* $\\pi .$ Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\ge \\pi $" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6bd67b3c-2b82-5f26-85f7-a27a12134ac9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bd67b3c-2b82-5f26-85f7-a27a12134ac9" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd67b3c-2b82-5f26-85f7-a27a12134ac9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0984546-b20e-5b16-9c6e-a0f235f5b648" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba9859b4-7393-5df1-a3c2-e0b992884a5c" "answer-de5c211d-af73-5bd4-ae3e-1c13d8266b71" "answer-d0984546-b20e-5b16-9c6e-a0f235f5b648" "answer-1265bd5a-78cd-5b77-952d-2c9a8def8f52" "answer-d9e38f9b-e8f7-5d70-99cf-388691c482ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba9859b4-7393-5df1-a3c2-e0b992884a5c" :answer/id #uuid "ba9859b4-7393-5df1-a3c2-e0b992884a5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x<\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de5c211d-af73-5bd4-ae3e-1c13d8266b71" :answer/id #uuid "de5c211d-af73-5bd4-ae3e-1c13d8266b71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0984546-b20e-5b16-9c6e-a0f235f5b648" :answer/id #uuid "d0984546-b20e-5b16-9c6e-a0f235f5b648" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge \\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1265bd5a-78cd-5b77-952d-2c9a8def8f52" :answer/id #uuid "1265bd5a-78cd-5b77-952d-2c9a8def8f52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le \\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9e38f9b-e8f7-5d70-99cf-388691c482ad" :answer/id #uuid "d9e38f9b-e8f7-5d70-99cf-388691c482ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≰\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8f075ba4-7815-572a-b12a-c4691f9f8fab" :question/id #uuid "8f075ba4-7815-572a-b12a-c4691f9f8fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-176028" :question/problem "$\\,$ $x\\ge y$\n\nThe predicate $P(x,y)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "In plain English, our predicate $P(x,y)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is greater than or equal to $y.$\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is greater than or equal to $y.$\n\nIn other words, $x$ is *smaller than* $y.$ Therefore, $¬P(x,y)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x<y$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a9fb1666-ac67-59fa-9a0b-88ff4f949f8d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a9fb1666-ac67-59fa-9a0b-88ff4f949f8d" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fb1666-ac67-59fa-9a0b-88ff4f949f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24b5f8c2-2b99-5562-891b-303dd931bed9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24b5f8c2-2b99-5562-891b-303dd931bed9" "answer-a428c3b8-01de-5e8f-b73b-74fada2e9234" "answer-4277be64-1b71-5d62-a369-59d7b2d5d193" "answer-a48dea4d-1fe6-5b8f-813a-9fa35ad0c35e" "answer-4c605d05-6b7a-5db3-b1e6-f476658f16d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24b5f8c2-2b99-5562-891b-303dd931bed9" :answer/id #uuid "24b5f8c2-2b99-5562-891b-303dd931bed9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x<y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a428c3b8-01de-5e8f-b73b-74fada2e9234" :answer/id #uuid "a428c3b8-01de-5e8f-b73b-74fada2e9234" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>\\frac{1}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4277be64-1b71-5d62-a369-59d7b2d5d193" :answer/id #uuid "4277be64-1b71-5d62-a369-59d7b2d5d193" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge -y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a48dea4d-1fe6-5b8f-813a-9fa35ad0c35e" :answer/id #uuid "a48dea4d-1fe6-5b8f-813a-9fa35ad0c35e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c605d05-6b7a-5db3-b1e6-f476658f16d3" :answer/id #uuid "4c605d05-6b7a-5db3-b1e6-f476658f16d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198708 :question/problem "Find the truth set of $¬P(x)$ given the following predicate defined over the universal set of positive integers between $1$ and $10,$ inclusive: \n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x$ is prime" :question/worked-solution "The truth set of $¬P(x)$ is the *complement* of the truth set of $P(x):$\n\n$$\n{T}_{¬P}={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P},\n$$\n\nThe truth set of $P(x)$ is the set of primes between $1$ and $10,$ inclusive, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\{2,3,5,7\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{¬P} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{\\{2,3,5,7\\}} \\\\  & =\\{1,4,6,8,9,10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186194874 :answer-field/correct "answer-58d76e8b-1a86-5ffa-acd2-1fa5e4dbcdfa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-58d76e8b-1a86-5ffa-acd2-1fa5e4dbcdfa" "answer-a8df4836-12c1-578d-98ab-34cb7a048b5a" "answer-c4deb8d6-b558-5a44-a9ac-edf5f9c74500"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58d76e8b-1a86-5ffa-acd2-1fa5e4dbcdfa" :answer/id #uuid "58d76e8b-1a86-5ffa-acd2-1fa5e4dbcdfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,4,6,8,9,10\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8df4836-12c1-578d-98ab-34cb7a048b5a" :answer/id #uuid "a8df4836-12c1-578d-98ab-34cb7a048b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{2,3,5,7\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4deb8d6-b558-5a44-a9ac-edf5f9c74500" :answer/id #uuid "c4deb8d6-b558-5a44-a9ac-edf5f9c74500" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f501796-fc0b-5879-8eb1-568ded583cc8" :question/id #uuid "2f501796-fc0b-5879-8eb1-568ded583cc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-175518" :question/problem "Find the truth set of $¬P(x)$ given the following predicate defined over the universal set $R:$\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x-7<-9$" :question/worked-solution "The truth set of $¬P(x)$ is the *complement* of the truth set of $P(x):$\n\n$$\n{T}_{¬P}={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\n$$\n\nTo find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-7 & <-9 \\\\ x & <-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is\n${T}_{P}=(-\\infty ,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{¬P} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{(-\\infty ,-2)} \\\\  & =[-2,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-756361aa-d5cf-55f5-9c74-7d766195b840"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-756361aa-d5cf-55f5-9c74-7d766195b840" :answer-field/id #uuid "756361aa-d5cf-55f5-9c74-7d766195b840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d8e47ef-6800-5c53-99fc-6fcc124ca2f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e0baf9c-dfdb-50b1-8d2c-f2950159bc02" "answer-54737426-e934-5b9e-a7e3-ee8d5c4c5497" "answer-18f25b9b-3d35-56ca-9327-f8285b1ec334" "answer-a6a130d7-695f-5eff-b612-a89637d42af1" "answer-6d8e47ef-6800-5c53-99fc-6fcc124ca2f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e0baf9c-dfdb-50b1-8d2c-f2950159bc02" :answer/id #uuid "2e0baf9c-dfdb-50b1-8d2c-f2950159bc02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54737426-e934-5b9e-a7e3-ee8d5c4c5497" :answer/id #uuid "54737426-e934-5b9e-a7e3-ee8d5c4c5497" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18f25b9b-3d35-56ca-9327-f8285b1ec334" :answer/id #uuid "18f25b9b-3d35-56ca-9327-f8285b1ec334" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6a130d7-695f-5eff-b612-a89637d42af1" :answer/id #uuid "a6a130d7-695f-5eff-b612-a89637d42af1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d8e47ef-6800-5c53-99fc-6fcc124ca2f5" :answer/id #uuid "6d8e47ef-6800-5c53-99fc-6fcc124ca2f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199522 :tutorial/content "The **negation** of the predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lnot P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nThis predicate is true if and only if $P$ is false for a specific set of assignment variables $x_1,x_2, \\ldots x_n.$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate defined over the universal set $\\mathbb Z.$\n\n$\\qquad$ *$P(m):$ $m$ is even*\n\nThen, $\\lnot P(m)$ is a new predicate that reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$m$ is **not** even*\n\nThis can be simplified as:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$m$ is odd*\n\nFor example:\n\n- $P(2)$ is true while $\\lnot P(2)$ is false\n\n- $P(3)$ is false while $\\lnot P(3)$ is true" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199543 :tutorial/content "Let $T_P$ be the truth set of the predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n).$\n\nFrom the definition of the negation of a predicate, we have that $\\lnot P$ is true if and only if $P$ is false. Therefore, the truth set of $\\neg P$ is the *complement* of the truth set of $P{:}$\n\n$$\nT_{\\neg P} = \\overline{T}_P\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n$P(x):$ $-2x +3 \\leq 5$\n\nTo find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & \\le 5 \\\\ -2x & \\le 2 \\\\ x & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = [-1,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{¬P} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{[-1,\\infty )} \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198515 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
